            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a punt, then the Latin for a raft."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: puppis, puppis",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "puppis, puppis — \"the stern (of a ship); a ship\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Gubernātor in puppī stat et nāvem regit. The helmsman stands at the stern and steers the ship."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Third declension, feminine, with accusative puppim and ablative puppī. English poop deck comes from it through French."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The origin of puppis is not settled: Lewis & Short call it doubtful and EtymDB gives no parent. English poop, the raised deck at the stern, comes from it through French poupe."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
