            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "prōra, prōrae — \"the prow, the bow (of a ship)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nauta in prōrā stat et lītus spectat. A sailor stands in the prow and watches the shore."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "First declension, feminine: prōra, prōrae f. Prōra et puppis, the whole of a thing, front to back. English prow comes from it through French."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "prōra was borrowed from Greek prōira 'prow' (EtymDB), built on the root of pro 'forward'. English prow comes from it through French proue."
            }
          ]
        },
