              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "carīna, carīnae — \"the keel (of a ship); a boat\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nāvis nova carīnam longam et validam habet. The new ship has a long, strong keel."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "First declension, feminine: carīna, carīnae f. First a nutshell, then the keel. English careen 'to tip a ship on its side' comes from it through French."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "carīna is inherited and is usually traced to PIE ḱerh₂- (EtymDB); Lewis & Short compare Greek karyon 'nut', since its first sense is a nutshell. English careen comes from it through French carène."
            }
          ]
        },
